### Why does a hot row hurt performance?

Every UPDATE takes a row-level lock. Thousands of concurrent increments queue on that one lock, running one at a time. The database is not CPU-bound; it is lock-bound.

```java
package com.bank.hotrow;

public class HotRowProblem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hot row problem:");
        System.out.println("  1000 concurrent UPDATEs on one counter row");
        System.out.println("  Each waits for the row lock -> serialized");
        System.out.println("  Throughput: ~100 updates/sec (lock contention)");
        System.out.println("  Database CPU: 10% (not the bottleneck!)");
        System.out.println("  The LOCK is the bottleneck");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Hot row problem:
  1000 concurrent UPDATEs on one counter row
  Each waits for the row lock -> serialized
  Throughput: ~100 updates/sec (lock contention)
  Database CPU: 10% (not the bottleneck!)
  The LOCK is the bottleneck
```

### How does counter sharding work?

Split one counter into N sub-rows. Each writer picks a shard at random. Read is SUM of all shards.

```java
package com.bank.hotrow;

import java.util.concurrent.ThreadLocalRandom;

public class CounterSharding {
    private static final int NUM_SHARDS = 16;
    private final int[] shards = new int[NUM_SHARDS];

    public void increment() {
        int shard = ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(NUM_SHARDS);
        shards[shard]++;
    }

    public int getTotal() {
        int total = 0;
        for (int s : shards) total += s;
        return total;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        CounterSharding counter = new CounterSharding();
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) counter.increment();
        System.out.println("Total across 16 shards: " + counter.getTotal());
    }
}
```

```sql
-- DB counter sharding
CREATE TABLE counter_shards (
    counter_id VARCHAR(50),
    shard_index INT,
    value BIGINT DEFAULT 0,
    PRIMARY KEY (counter_id, shard_index)
);

-- Write: pick random shard
UPDATE counter_shards SET value = value + 1
WHERE counter_id = 'fd-opened-today' AND shard_index = 7;

-- Read: sum all shards
SELECT SUM(value) FROM counter_shards WHERE counter_id = 'fd-opened-today';
```

```text
Expected output:
Total across 16 shards: 1000

With 16 shards: 16 writers proceed in parallel
Contention reduced ~16x
Read: SUM query over 16 rows
```

### How does Redis buffering work?

Accumulate increments in Redis with atomic INCR. A background job flushes the aggregate to the DB.

```java
package com.bank.hotrow;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.scheduling.annotation.Scheduled;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class RedisBufferedCounter {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final CounterRepository dbRepo;
    private static final String KEY = "counter:fd-opened-today";

    public RedisBufferedCounter(StringRedisTemplate redis, CounterRepository dbRepo) {
        this.redis = redis;
        this.dbRepo = dbRepo;
    }

    public void increment() {
        redis.opsForValue().increment(KEY);
        // Atomic INCR: handles thousands/sec on single key
    }

    @Scheduled(fixedRate = 5000) // every 5 seconds
    public void flushToDb() {
        String value = redis.opsForValue().getAndSet(KEY, "0");
        if (value != null && !"0".equals(value)) {
            long delta = Long.parseLong(value);
            dbRepo.incrementBy("fd-opened-today", delta);
            System.out.println("Flushed " + delta + " to database");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Flushed 5432 to database

10,000 increments/sec -> 1 DB write every 5 seconds
DB contention: nearly eliminated
Trade-off: DB stale between flushes (serve reads from Redis)
```

### How do sharding and buffering compare?

| Aspect | Counter Sharding | Redis Buffering |
|---|---|---|
| Durability | Every increment durable in DB | Un-flushed delta at risk |
| Read freshness | Eventually consistent | Fresh from Redis, stale in DB |
| Max throughput | Limited by N shards | Nearly unlimited (Redis single-thread) |
| Best for | Durable counters | Approximate counters (likes, views) |

```java
package com.bank.hotrow;

public class ShardingVsBuffering {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Counter sharding: every increment in DB, less contention");
        System.out.println("Redis buffering: extreme throughput, DB stale between flushes");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Composable: sharded Redis counters flushed to DB");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Counter sharding: every increment in DB, less contention
Redis buffering: extreme throughput, DB stale between flushes

Composable: sharded Redis counters flushed to DB
```

### What about real money? Avoid the hot row entirely.

For account balances, model each change as an appended ledger entry. The balance is the SUM. No hot row.

```java
package com.bank.hotrow;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class AppendOnlyLedger {
    // WRONG: single balance row updated on every transaction
    // UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 50000 WHERE id = 'SAV2001'
    // -> HOT ROW under load

    // CORRECT: append-only ledger entries
    // INSERT INTO ledger (account_id, amount, type, description)
    // VALUES ('SAV2001', -50000, 'DEBIT', 'Transfer to Priya')
    // Balance = SUM(amount) WHERE account_id = 'SAV2001'

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("For real money: NO hot shared counter");
        System.out.println("  Model: append-only ledger entries");
        System.out.println("  Balance = SUM of all entries for the account");
        System.out.println("  No single row updated by every transaction");
        System.out.println("  Maintains full ACID and audit trail");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
For real money: NO hot shared counter
  Model: append-only ledger entries
  Balance = SUM of all entries for the account
  No single row updated by every transaction
  Maintains full ACID and audit trail
```

### What are quick wins without sharding?

Keep transactions tiny so the lock is held briefly. Use atomic increment.

```sql
-- Keep the transaction minimal: just the increment
BEGIN;
UPDATE counter SET value = value + 1 WHERE id = 'fd-opened-today';
COMMIT;
-- Lock held for ~1ms instead of 100ms with business logic in the transaction
```

```java
package com.bank.hotrow;

public class QuickWins {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Quick wins before sharding:");
        System.out.println("  1. Atomic increment (no SELECT + UPDATE, just UPDATE +1)");
        System.out.println("  2. Minimal transaction (only the counter in the TX)");
        System.out.println("  3. Batch increments at app layer (batch 100 into 1 update)");
        System.out.println("  4. If these are not enough: shard or buffer");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Quick wins before sharding:
  1. Atomic increment (no SELECT + UPDATE, just UPDATE +1)
  2. Minimal transaction (only the counter in the TX)
  3. Batch increments at app layer (batch 100 into 1 update)
  4. If these are not enough: shard or buffer
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

For real money, avoid the hot row entirely with an append-only ledger. For approximate counters, shard or buffer. They compose.

```text
Senior guidelines:
  [x] Real money: append-only ledger, NOT a shared counter
  [x] Durable counter: shard into N sub-rows
  [x] Approximate counter: buffer in Redis, flush periodically
  [x] Composable: sharded Redis counters flushed to DB
  [x] Quick win: atomic increment in minimal transaction
  [x] Monitor: row lock wait time per hot table
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using SELECT then UPDATE instead of atomic increment, which doubles the lock time and introduces a race condition. Another is sharding a counter for real money instead of using an append-only ledger.

```java
package com.bank.hotrow;

public class HotRowMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: SELECT + UPDATE (two operations, race condition)
        System.out.println("WRONG: SELECT balance, then UPDATE balance = balance - amount");
        System.out.println("  Two transactions read 100000, both set to 50000");
        System.out.println("  Lost update: 50000 lost!");

        // CORRECT: atomic UPDATE
        System.out.println("CORRECT: UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 50000");
        System.out.println("  Single atomic operation, no lost update");

        // WRONG: sharded counter for real money
        System.out.println("WRONG: sharded counter for account balance");
        System.out.println("  Partial reads, no ACID guarantee across shards");

        // CORRECT: append-only ledger for money
        System.out.println("CORRECT: append-only ledger entries for money");
        System.out.println("  Full audit trail, SUM for balance, no hot row");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: SELECT balance, then UPDATE balance = balance - amount
  Two transactions read 100000, both set to 50000
  Lost update: 50000 lost!
CORRECT: UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 50000
  Single atomic operation, no lost update
WRONG: sharded counter for account balance
  Partial reads, no ACID guarantee across shards
CORRECT: append-only ledger entries for money
  Full audit trail, SUM for balance, no hot row
```
